rlm-fe

Fuzzy-edit. Pick one or more files reachable from the current
directory (recursive) using fzf, then open them in $EDITOR.

KEYS  (also listed in the picker's header line)
  Tab            mark a file for multi-select
  Enter          open the marked files (or the current one) in $EDITOR
  Shift-Up/Down  scroll the preview one line
  Alt-Up/Down    scroll the preview half a page
  Ctrl-P         cycle preview pane height (40% / 70% / hidden)
  Ctrl-G         abort

Shift-Up/Down are fzf's own defaults, bound explicitly here so the
header cannot drift from what the picker actually does. The half-page
Alt bindings are added because a source file is usually longer than
the pane. Ctrl-U/Ctrl-D were deliberately not used: fzf binds those to
unix-line-discard and delete-char/eof on the query line, which are
worth keeping.

Files are listed with fd, including hidden and gitignored files (the
.git directory is excluded). The first column is a humanized age
("2 hours ago"); the second is the path relative to the current
directory. The full absolute path is what the picker acts on.

Any arguments are joined with spaces and used to prepopulate the
fzf query, e.g. `fe foo` opens the picker with "foo" already typed.

SORTING
Entries form a single timeline, newest first, so the visible order
always matches the visible age column. Each file's change time is:

  dirty / untracked   filesystem mtime  (when you actually edited it)
  clean, tracked      git commit time   (when it last really changed)
  not in git at all   filesystem mtime

That time is then taken against the MRU pick time, so opening a file
floats it to the top and it decays from there, interleaved with
everything else. There is no separate pinned block of picked files.

The dirty/clean split matters because in a fresh clone every file's
filesystem mtime is the *checkout* timestamp, not an edit time: 500
sampled files in one work checkout shared just 2 distinct mtimes.
Sorting on mtime alone (or on max(mtime, commit time)) would stamp the
whole tree with one meaningless value. A file's mtime only means
"someone edited this" when git reports it as modified, staged, or
untracked, which is exactly when it should win over the commit time.
Dirty state comes from one `git status --porcelain -z` per run.

Git times come from one `git log --name-only` walk that resolves every
tracked file in a single process. The walk runs with
GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1: in a partial clone (--filter=blob:none) git would
otherwise fetch missing objects over the network one round-trip at a
time, which turns a 0.1s walk into minutes. With lazy fetch refused
git stops at the first missing object; files it could not resolve fall
back to their filesystem mtime. Outside a git repo the git step is
skipped entirely.

PREVIEW
The preview pane is rendered by bin/fe-preview and shows the full
path, a GitHub permalink to the file, the filesystem mtime (tagged
[dirty XY] or [untracked] when git says so, which is when that mtime
is the authoritative change time), the last commit (time, short SHA,
subject), its author as "Name <email>", the associated pull request
(number and title) as a clickable link, and then the file contents
(via bat when available, else cat).

The author is read with git's %aN <%aE>, the mailmap-aware forms, so a
GitHub noreply address is rewritten to the person's real one when a
.mailmap applies (the work checkouts set mailmap.file in
~/git/work/.gitconfig). The lowercase %an/%ae would show an address you
cannot mail.

The GitHub line is an OSC 8 hyperlink pinned to the commit that last
touched that file, not to HEAD: that is the revision being previewed,
and it is far likelier to exist on the remote than a local HEAD
sitting on unpushed work. It is emitted only when the commit is
actually reachable from a remote branch, so a local-only commit shows
no link rather than one that 404s. Non-GitHub remotes are skipped. For
a dirty file the link is marked "(committed version)", since the
on-disk contents differ from what it points at. The visible label is
the repo-relative path, elided in the middle when long (src/…/Foo.java)
— a wrapped OSC 8 label breaks the click target, and the full path is
already on the Path: line where wrapping is harmless.

The PR number is parsed out of the commit subject first, matching the
two GitHub merge styles at their anchors:

  "<title> (#123)"                  squash-merge — subject IS the title,
                                    so number and title both come free
  "Merge pull request #123 from X"  merge commit — carries only the
                                    source branch, never the title

A bare "#123" anywhere else ("Fix #4242 in the parser") is an issue
reference, not a merge, and is deliberately ignored.

The GitHub API is consulted only when that leaves something missing —
no number at all, or a merge commit whose title is still unknown — and
the result is cached under ~/.cache/rlm-fe/pr/<sha> as
"<number>\t<title>\t<url>". A negative result is cached too (empty
file), so a commit genuinely without a PR costs one call once rather
than one per preview. A failed or empty lookup never discards a number
already parsed from the subject: "#123" with no title beats no PR line
at all.

Only the "#123" token is the hyperlink; the title follows as plain
text. A short, fixed-width click target can never wrap (which would
break the link) and makes the clickable region obvious instead of
turning a whole sentence into one link — and the title, being plain
text, is free to wrap harmlessly at any length.

MRU history is recorded by the shared helper _rlm-fe-history in
~/.cache/rlm-fe/history.tsv, keyed on each file's full absolute path.
Because the cache stores full paths, a file keeps its recency no
matter which directory rlm-fe is run from.

ENVIRONMENT
  EDITOR   Editor to open the selected files (falls back to vi).

REQUIRES
  fd, fzf, python3. Optional: git (commit times, author, PR),
  gh (PR lookup when the subject has no number), bat (syntax
  highlighting in the preview).
